22:03:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s understandable to feel nervous, especially in a field that can still be male-dominated. But she doesn’t need to become “one of the guys” or change her personality to belong—engineering needs all kinds of people and perspectives. A few practical tips: - **Find her people early.** Look for women-in-engineering groups, engineering societies, affinity groups, project teams, or mentorship programs. Having even a few friendly classmates can make a huge difference. - **Get involved in hands-on groups.** Design teams, robotics clubs, hackathons, research labs, maker spaces, and professional chapters give people something concrete to work on together, which often makes socializing easier. - **Ask questions without apologizing.** Everyone is learning. “Can you walk me through how you got that?” is a normal and useful engineering question—not a sign she doesn’t belong. - **Build relationships with classmates and instructors.** A study group, office hours, or one supportive professor/TA can help both academically and socially. - **Be alert to disrespect, but don’t internalize it.** If someone is dismissive or makes sexist comments, that reflects on them and the environment—not on her abilities. Document serious incidents and use school channels, mentors, or trusted faculty if needed. - **Remember that “nerdy” is not one personality type.** Plenty of engineers are creative, athletic, social, artistic, quiet, outgoing, practical, or eccentric. She’ll likely meet people she genuinely clicks with. - **Focus on her own reasons for being there.** Curiosity, problem-solving, building things, helping people—whatever motivates her can anchor her when she feels out of place. Most importantly: fitting in shouldn’t mean tolerating exclusion. A good engineering community should make room for her as she is.
