22:03:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: In some engineering programs, that description is honestly pretty recognizable: lots of men, lots of laptops, gaming talk, technical hobbies, and competitive problem-solving. “Nerdy” isn’t necessarily inaccurate as a shorthand for that campus culture. But two things can both be true: 1. **She may encounter plenty of guys like that.** 2. **That still won’t be the whole student body, and she doesn’t have to share every interest to work and socialize well with them.** Engineering is often easier socially than it looks from the outside because people bond over projects, labs, deadlines, internships, and complaining about difficult classes—not only video games or math puzzles. She can be friendly and collaborative without pretending to care about gaming or adopting anyone else’s personality. It may help her to proactively seek the parts of engineering culture that feel more like her: women-in-engineering groups, multidisciplinary design teams, biomedical/environmental/human-centered projects, outreach groups, entrepreneurship, or clubs unrelated to engineering altogether. Her closest friends do not all need to be engineers. And if the issue is less “they’re nerdy” and more “I’m worried I’ll be treated as an outsider,” that’s a real concern in some spaces. Finding supportive peers, mentors, and faculty early is the best protection against feeling isolated.
