22:03:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re not imagining where the impression comes from. Engineering has historically been male-dominated, and many workplaces do include people who are very technical, introverted, into games, gadgets, or puzzles. Your uncle’s experience may reflect that, especially depending on his industry and generation. But TV tends to compress that into a caricature: “awkward guy with glasses” is an easy visual stereotype, not a reliable description of who engineers are or how they act. Mechanical engineering, for example, includes people doing manufacturing, automotive, energy, product design, aerospace, robotics, consulting, sales, operations, and more—many of whom are highly social and work constantly with technicians, clients, designers, managers, and cross-functional teams. For your sister, the useful takeaway isn’t “the stereotype is totally false.” It’s: she may be entering a culture with more men and more technical hobbies than average, so she can plan for that without assuming she’ll have no peers or that she has to perform a different personality. She can: - seek women/nonbinary engineering networks and mentoring; - join project teams where collaboration matters; - make friends across majors; - choose internships and teams partly based on culture; - set boundaries if jokes or behavior become disrespectful; - keep her own interests and style rather than treating them as incompatible with engineering. Being comfortable around people with different interests is a useful skill. So is choosing environments that respect her.
