22:03:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s fair to say engineering tends to attract people who enjoy technical problem-solving, math, systems, and building or understanding how things work. If you mean “nerdy” in that broad, non-insulting sense, then yes—those interests are central to the work, and the field will likely always contain many people who have them. Where it becomes less useful is treating that shared interest as a prediction of everyone’s personality, social skills, or whether your sister can belong. Enjoying machinery and math doesn’t automatically mean someone is awkward, sexist, unfriendly, or impossible to connect with. And she can enjoy engineering for her own reasons—design, creativity, impact, stability, teamwork, or curiosity—without matching a stereotype. So the practical advice stays pretty simple: expect a technical culture, find communities and project teams that feel welcoming, and let her decide which kinds of people and workplaces she wants around her.
